{
  "$schema": "https://www.getbifrost.ai/schema",
  "client": {
    "_comment_drop_excess": "2026-08-10: false -> true. With false, a full provider buffer QUEUES instead of rejecting, so requests sat in Bifrost's buffer for hours (observed: 3.9h on /v1/chat/completions, 231 leaked CLOSE_WAIT sockets, 4300 open FDs). true makes an over-capacity lane return immediately so the caller's fallback chain cascades to a cloud provider. Nothing may queue unbounded.",
    "drop_excess_requests": true,
    "initial_pool_size": 100,
    "enforce_auth_on_inference": true
  },
  "providers": {
    "vllm-local": {
      "keys": [
        {
          "name": "local-noauth",
          "value": "EMPTY",
          "_comment_models": "Pass-9 (2026-08-31, Migration-20): the engine now serves ONE name, qwen3.8-27b (syv-ai stack, unmodified vLLM launch). The 8 legacy names stay in `models` because ~350 Legion/Zero/ADA call sites still send them, and each is remapped to qwen3.8-27b in `aliases` before forwarding -- so every response's model field now echoes the TRUE model. qwen3-chat remains the stable ROLE alias for new code. After ANY change here: restart shared-bifrost FIRST (imports config.json at boot), THEN run `python bifrost/sync_vk_allowlists.py` -- reverse order silently syncs the stale list (this bug bit on Pass-6 AND Pass-7).",
          "models": [
            "Qwen3.5-35B-A3B",
            "Qwen3.6-35B-A3B",
            "qwen3-chat",
            "Qwen3-32B-AWQ",
            "gpt-oss-20b",
            "Qwen3.6-27B",
            "nemotron-3.5-lightning",
            "local-chat",
            "qwen3.8-27b"
          ],
          "weight": 1.0,
          "aliases": {
            "Qwen3.5-35B-A3B": "qwen3.8-27b",
            "Qwen3.6-35B-A3B": "qwen3.8-27b",
            "qwen3-chat": "qwen3.8-27b",
            "Qwen3-32B-AWQ": "qwen3.8-27b",
            "gpt-oss-20b": "qwen3.8-27b",
            "Qwen3.6-27B": "qwen3.8-27b",
            "nemotron-3.5-lightning": "qwen3.8-27b",
            "local-chat": "qwen3.8-27b"
          }
        }
      ],
      "network_config": {
        "base_url": "http://qwen38-chat:18020",
        "default_request_timeout_in_seconds": 120,
        "max_retries": 1
      },
      "custom_provider_config": {
        "base_provider_type": "openai",
        "allowed_requests": {
          "_comment_list_models": "MUST stay false. /v1/models fans out to every provider with list_models=true, so a model-list call joins the inference queue behind real work (Legion's stack_monitor.py documented it hanging past 150s on 2026-08-03, when vllm-chat ran --max-num-seqs 1). vllm-chat now runs --max-num-seqs 6, which makes the hang less likely but NOT impossible \u2014 model discovery must never depend on inference capacity, at any concurrency. Corrected 2026-08-11: this comment previously asserted seqs=1 as current fact.",
          "list_models": false,
          "chat_completion": true,
          "chat_completion_stream": true
        }
      },
      "_comment_capacity": "CORRECTED 2026-08-15 (this entry described Pass-6's Qwen3-8B-AWQ state as current fact; it was superseded THE SAME DAY by Pass-7). Current: 2026-08-11 Pass-7 swapped vllm-chat to nvidia/NVIDIA-Nemotron-3.5-Lightning-30B-A3B-NVFP4 (30B total / 3B active MoE), max-num-seqs held at 6. vLLM's boot-time budget check measured 'Maximum concurrency for 32,768 tokens per request: 5.03x' (164,659 KV tokens off 2.43 GiB KV headroom after 17.86 GiB of weights) -- lower than Pass-6's 8B (9.38x) because the weights are ~3.2x bigger, but still comfortably above the 6 configured seqs in practice because real ADA/Legion/Zero traffic rarely holds a full 32K context per concurrent request (measured 24h max input 7,433 tokens; num_preemptions_total held at 0 under a 10-concurrent/1,473-avg-token-prompt load test). Bifrost concurrency stays 6, buffer_size RAISED 12->16 + default_request_timeout_in_seconds RAISED 90->120 by Wave5-WS2A (2026-08-16, Feature-1000532) to absorb market-hours burst without tripping ADA's local circuit breaker as fast (see ADA .claude/rules/50-llm.md + backend/infrastructure/llm_router.py _LOCAL_CB_THRESHOLD 5->8) -- so callers don't get 400 drops before vLLM's own queue is engaged. If this MoE model wedges under sustained real load, drop concurrency back to 1 / buffer 2 first (matches a vllm --max-num-seqs=1 rollback in shared-infra/.env) before touching anything else. Live GPU check 2026-08-15: 31,531 / 32,607 MiB used (96.7%), 77% util under real traffic -- see ADA .claude/rules/50-llm.md for the full VRAM math and upgrade-eval writeup.",
      "_comment_capacity_2026_08_19": "buffer_size RAISED 16->64 (concurrency held at 6 -- that is vLLM's real max-num-seqs and raising it just moves the queue into the engine as preemption). WHY: until today the local model was a rung nothing actually reached, because every ladder in ADA was cloud-only. On 2026-08-19 the entire free cloud tier saturated as ONE event (cerebras 402 credits-out, openrouter :free 429 free-models-per-day, the openrouter key 403 owner-daily-limit, nvidia-nim 429, groq 429 token-cap) and ADA added vllm-local as the structural terminal rung on every non-grade ladder. That turned local from ~0 qps into the fallback for every starved lane at once, and a 16-deep buffer overflowed instantly -- callers got 'request dropped: queue is full' 400s, which reads exactly like a model outage. 64 gives ~4x burst headroom; ADA's own 90s per-call ladder timeout bounds how long anything sits in it. Rollback ladder unchanged: if the MoE wedges under sustained load, drop concurrency to 1 / buffer 2 (with a matching vllm --max-num-seqs=1 in shared-infra/.env) BEFORE touching anything else.",
      "concurrency_and_buffer_size": {
        "concurrency": 32,
        "buffer_size": 256
      },
      "_comment_capacity_2026_08_31": "Pass-9 (Migration-20, Legion 14397): vllm-chat (Nemotron, max-num-seqs 6) replaced by qwen38-chat -- the syv-ai/qwen38-27b-rtx3090 patched-vLLM stack serving Qwen3.8-27B W4A16 on port 18020, batch profile, MAX_SEQS=32, MAX_LEN=65536, GPU_UTIL=0.82 (coexists with vllm-embed). Concurrency raised 6->32 to match the engine's real seq slots; buffer 64->256 (~8x burst headroom vs the measured 86,943 queue-full drops/week on the 6-slot engine). ALL model-name aliasing now lives in this key's aliases dict: the engine serves ONE honest name (qwen3.8-27b) and every legacy alias is remapped here, so response model fields stop echoing Qwen3.5-35B-A3B (a model this box has not run since 2026-08-11). Rollback: base_url back to http://vllm-chat:8000, concurrency 6/buffer 64, restore the previous aliases dict (git history of this file), start vllm-chat compose service."
    },
